WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.255 --> 00:00:03.262
- When I'm out there riding my bike

00:00:03.262 --> 00:00:05.427
and I have this pump on,

00:00:05.427 --> 00:00:07.890
it's almost like it's not even there.

00:00:07.890 --> 00:00:10.490
I just feel the freedom of being out there

00:00:10.490 --> 00:00:13.330
and feeling the speed and adrenaline.

00:00:13.330 --> 00:00:16.877
It's just Hannah out
there riding her bike.

00:00:16.877 --> 00:00:19.300
Some might say I'm like Dora the Explorer,

00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:22.768
'cause I'm always wearing this backpack.

00:00:22.768 --> 00:00:25.518
But this backpack keeps me alive.

00:00:30.896 --> 00:00:34.346
My name is Hannah Jordan;
I'm from Tulsa, Oklahoma.

00:00:34.346 --> 00:00:38.061
I'm 15 years old, and I'm
a competitive cyclist.

00:00:38.061 --> 00:00:40.745
My long-term goals are to become pro

00:00:40.745 --> 00:00:44.102
and be in the Olympics in 2020 for Tokyo

00:00:44.102 --> 00:00:48.489
and bring back the the
Tour de France for women.

00:00:48.489 --> 00:00:51.383
I have an unknown rare metabolic disease.

00:00:51.383 --> 00:00:54.324
From when I was very little, I was
in and out of the hospital

00:00:54.324 --> 00:00:56.532
and very sick all the time.

00:00:56.532 --> 00:01:01.200
And, when I was nine, they gave me
only a couple months to live.

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:04.643
So like, I sorta skipped childhood.

00:01:04.643 --> 00:01:06.945
My mom took me all over the country

00:01:06.945 --> 00:01:10.359
to some specialists and
they found a solution

00:01:10.359 --> 00:01:12.787
and they gave me a thing called a G-Tube

00:01:12.787 --> 00:01:15.709
that pumps sugar into my stomach.

00:01:15.709 --> 00:01:20.120
It basically keeps my blood
sugar at a normal rate.

00:01:20.120 --> 00:01:22.694
I always wanna do competitive stuff.

00:01:22.694 --> 00:01:24.899
For most of my life,
I couldn't play sports

00:01:24.899 --> 00:01:26.597
because of my condition.

00:01:26.597 --> 00:01:28.955
When I got on the bike, it was almost like

00:01:28.955 --> 00:01:30.543
my problems weren't there.

00:01:30.543 --> 00:01:34.405
It's just been like a whole
new world opened up to me.

00:01:34.405 --> 00:01:35.813
- This one right here is really special.

00:01:35.813 --> 00:01:37.744
This was her first one.

00:01:37.744 --> 00:01:39.298
And it's a silver medal,
but she'd actually

00:01:39.298 --> 00:01:41.766
only been riding a bike two months.

00:01:41.766 --> 00:01:44.743
And then shortly after
that, she did this one.

00:01:44.743 --> 00:01:47.672
She did 101 miles in five
hours and 18 minutes,

00:01:47.672 --> 00:01:49.801
which is very, very fast.

00:01:49.801 --> 00:01:51.009
And that's kinda when we realized

00:01:51.009 --> 00:01:55.697
that she probably was gonna
be pretty good at this.

00:01:55.697 --> 00:01:56.934
- [Hannah] This weekend,

00:01:56.934 --> 00:02:00.717
I will be competing in Tulsa
Tough, a three day race.

00:02:00.717 --> 00:02:04.145
Each day, 1,000 racers compete
from six different countries

00:02:04.145 --> 00:02:05.475
around the world.

00:02:05.475 --> 00:02:08.558
These women are usually 20 and above.

00:02:14.801 --> 00:02:16.835
My mom and my dad, they're
my biggest supporters.

00:02:16.835 --> 00:02:18.340
They take me to all the races,

00:02:18.340 --> 00:02:21.477
and help me make it in cycling.

00:02:21.477 --> 00:02:23.893
My mom, she helps me get everything ready.

00:02:23.893 --> 00:02:25.689
Then my dad, at the 200 meter mark,

00:02:25.689 --> 00:02:27.113
he starts yelling.

00:02:27.113 --> 00:02:28.686
- Let's go Hannah, let's go, go!

00:02:28.686 --> 00:02:29.880
- [Hannah] It's like a mental marker,

00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:32.544
of like when I start needing to sprint.

00:02:32.544 --> 00:02:34.529
What makes me different
from other cyclists

00:02:34.529 --> 00:02:36.866
is the ability to tap in that willpower

00:02:36.866 --> 00:02:39.058
and determination.

00:02:39.058 --> 00:02:41.841
I like to tell people it's
sort of coded in my DNA

00:02:41.841 --> 00:02:43.557
to be a fighter.

00:02:43.557 --> 00:02:45.541
Anyone that's been in the hospital

00:02:45.541 --> 00:02:48.109
for a long time, or has
gone through some struggles,

00:02:48.109 --> 00:02:49.848
or pain, or suffering,

00:02:49.848 --> 00:02:52.675
they just know how to dig deep and tap in

00:02:52.675 --> 00:02:54.648
and to be resilient.

00:02:54.648 --> 00:02:57.115
And even if you lose some days

00:02:57.115 --> 00:02:58.190
and you win some others,

00:02:58.190 --> 00:02:59.849
you have to keep getting back up,

00:02:59.849 --> 00:03:01.098
each and every single time,

00:03:01.098 --> 00:03:04.015
to go out there and ride your bike.

